    "manifest": {
      "title": "Zero velocity at the top is not zero acceleration",
      "summary": "A tossed ball rises, stops for an instant at its top and falls, while a held ball is released from rest at that same instant. Both carry velocity and acceleration arrows, and the acceleration stays -9.8 m/s^2 throughout free flight.",
      "assumptions": [
        "Constant downward acceleration of 9.8 m/s^2, no air resistance, up is positive.",
        "The held ball is supported by the gate (zero acceleration) until the apex instant, then falls freely."
      ],
      "description": "On the left a tossed ball rises from a launch mark to a dashed top line and falls back. On the right a held ball rests on a release gate at launch height; the gate opens at the instant the tossed ball reaches its top. Each ball has a velocity arrow v that shrinks to a dot at the top and then points down, and a downward acceleration arrow a of constant length in free flight. A height scale in metres runs beside the scene.",
